from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(2,'matrix','Matrix shapes and normalized batch similarity',
'''Embedding search can score many documents at once with a matrix product. If queries have shape [q,d] and documents [n,d], normalize each row and multiply queries by documents transposed to obtain [q,n]. Each output row is one query's score against every document.\n\nThe shared dimension d is a contract, not a value that can be reshaped arbitrarily. A transpose changes axes; reshape reorganizes the same flat storage. Confusing them can return the right dimensions with the wrong numerical relationships.''',
'''Compute row norms with axis=1 and keepdims=True, then divide using a zero-safe output. This broadcasts one norm per row across its d coordinates. Matrix multiplication @ performs a dot product along the shared dimension; elementwise * does not aggregate.\n\nFor queries [[1,0],[0,1]] and documents [[1,0],[1,1]], normalized scores are [[1,.707],[0,.707]]. Test against the manual cosine calculation before trusting a large batch. Keep dtype and finite-value checks at the boundary.''',
'''Normalizing the whole matrix by one global norm changes ranking assumptions. Dividing integer arrays in place can fail or truncate under an inappropriate dtype. An empty document collection requires a correctly shaped empty score matrix, not an index error.\n\nCheck dimension mismatch, zero rows, non-finite values and each result's query/document identity. Use small hand-computed matrices as an independent oracle.''',
'''Vectorized scoring is useful for transparent exact-retrieval reference results. Measure its runtime separately from embedding generation and compare numerical results with the single-vector implementation.\n\nFor SDETs, shapes and mathematical invariants are powerful tests: normalized nonzero rows have norm one, self-similarity is one and orthogonal similarity is zero. They can detect wrong axes without requiring a large model or provider account.''',
"import numpy as np\nq=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]])\nd=np.array([[1.,0.],[1.,1.]])\nd=d/np.linalg.norm(d,axis=1,keepdims=True)\nprint(q@d.T)",
"def batch_cosine(queries,docs):\n    import numpy as np\n    q=np.asarray(queries,dtype=float);d=np.asarray(docs,dtype=float)\n    if q.ndim!=2 or d.ndim!=2 or q.shape[1]!=d.shape[1]:raise ValueError('shape')\n    qn=np.linalg.norm(q,axis=1,keepdims=True);dn=np.linalg.norm(d,axis=1,keepdims=True)\n    q=np.divide(q,qn,out=np.zeros_like(q),where=qn!=0);d=np.divide(d,dn,out=np.zeros_like(d),where=dn!=0)\n    return (q@d.T).tolist()",
"import math\nr=batch_cosine([[1,0],[0,1]],[[1,0],[1,1]])\nassert r[0][0]==1 and r[1][0]==0\nassert abs(r[0][1]-1/math.sqrt(2))<1e-9\nassert batch_cosine([[0,0]],[[1,2]])==[[0]]","def batch_cosine(queries,docs):\n    return []",
('Which result shape scores q queries against n documents?',['[q,n]','[d,d]','[n,q,d]'],0,'The shared embedding dimension is contracted during matrix multiplication.'),['Matrix multiplication','Broadcasting'])
lab(8,'positions','Position information and causal invariance tests',
'''A sequence model must distinguish database failed after login from login failed after database. Token embeddings identify symbols, while positional mechanisms encode their order. Sinusoidal encodings use different frequencies by dimension; learned positional embeddings use a trained table; rotary embeddings transform queries and keys to express relative position relationships.\n\nThese mechanisms have different extrapolation behavior and configuration. A larger advertised context window is not achieved by simply appending zeros to the positional table.''',
'''For a simple sinusoidal demonstration, encode even coordinates with sin(pos/10000^(2i/d)) and odd coordinates with cos of the same angle. Add the position vector to the token representation in the classic formulation. Rotary mechanisms instead rotate paired coordinates in attention projections.\n\nTo test a causal decoder, compare early-position outputs before and after changing a later token in eval mode. Their equality within numerical tolerance verifies the mask's information-flow contract. Padding tests similarly perturb only masked positions.''',
'''A decoder's triangular mask is inverted, exposing future tokens. The test uses dropout in training mode and incorrectly attributes random differences to the mask. An index off-by-one assigns the same position to two adjacent tokens.\n\nControl random state and evaluation mode, inspect the permitted-position matrix and test sequences of several lengths including one. Shape tests alone do not detect future-information leakage.''',
'''Keep positional configuration and tokenizer revision with model artifacts. Report how truncation and longer sequences affect task quality rather than assuming every allowed position is equally useful.\n\nAn SDET can test position mechanisms with invariants: different positions have different encodings in the valid range, output dimensions are stable and future perturbations cannot influence earlier causal outputs. These checks isolate architecture behavior from semantic answer evaluation.''',
"import math\ndef position(pos):\n    return [math.sin(pos),math.cos(pos),math.sin(pos/100),math.cos(pos/100)]\nassert position(0)==[0.,1.,0.,1.]",
"def causal_allowed(query_position,key_position):\n    if min(query_position,key_position)<0:raise ValueError('position')\n    return key_position<=query_position",
"assert causal_allowed(2,0)\nassert causal_allowed(2,2)\nassert not causal_allowed(2,3)\ntry: causal_allowed(-1,0);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def causal_allowed(query_position,key_position):\n    return False",
('What invalidates a causal information-flow comparison?',['Uncontrolled dropout during the comparison','Evaluation mode','An explicit future-token perturbation'],0,'Random dropout can change outputs independently of the mask and hide the intended invariant.'),['Positional encoding','Rotary embedding'])
lab(13,'index-version','Embedding compatibility, batching and multilingual evaluation',
'''Equal vector dimensions do not imply compatible geometry. Two encoders can both produce 384 coordinates while mapping text into unrelated spaces. Mixing their document vectors or querying one index with the other encoder makes distance meaningless. Version the model revision, preprocessing, prefixes, pooling and normalization as one embedding contract.\n\nMultilingual capability is a task requirement to test, not a property to infer from a model name. Error IDs and code snippets may also behave differently from natural-language paraphrases.''',
'''Batch text in a stable order with IDs, bound batch tokens and preserve per-item failures. Store a manifest beside each index generation. During an upgrade, build a new index, evaluate paired queries and switch atomically while retaining a compatible old generation for rollback.\n\nFor a query/document encoder, use the documented role prefixes. Evaluate exact codes, paraphrases, multilingual requests and ambiguous failure signatures. Check model revision and dimensions before issuing a query.''',
'''A batch provider returns fewer vectors than inputs and the caller zips them silently, assigning later vectors to wrong IDs. An index upgrade changes normalization but keeps the old distance configuration. A cache reuses embeddings across preprocessing revisions.\n\nTest cardinality, ID/order preservation, finite values, manifest equality and failed-item retry. Use separate cache keys for model and preprocessing contracts.''',
'''Release evidence compares retrieval Recall@K and latency by query slice before and after re-embedding. Report the cost of the full corpus migration and how long both indexes are retained.\n\nSDETs should reject incompatible manifests deterministically before evaluating quality. A quality benchmark over mixed revisions cannot provide meaningful evidence about either encoder.''',
"contract={'model':'encoder-a@revision','dimension':384,'normalize':True,'prefix':'document:'}\nassert contract['dimension']>0",
"def compatible_embeddings(left,right):\n    keys=('model','dimension','normalize','prefix')\n    return all(k in left and k in right and left[k]==right[k] for k in keys)",
"a={'model':'a','dimension':3,'normalize':True,'prefix':'doc:'}\nassert compatible_embeddings(a,a)\nassert not compatible_embeddings(a,{**a,'model':'b'})\nassert not compatible_embeddings(a,{**a,'normalize':False})","def compatible_embeddings(left,right):\n    return False",
('What should happen to a query with an incompatible encoder manifest?',['Reject or route to its matching index','Search because dimensions match','Pad vectors to fit'],0,'Geometry compatibility includes revision and preprocessing, not only vector size.'),['Embedding version','Embedding batching'])
lab(14,'persistence','Qdrant persistence and payload-filter integration tests',
'''A vector database's operational behavior matters beyond nearest-neighbor arithmetic. Stable IDs make upserts replace the intended point, payloads provide tenant and source metadata, and persistence determines whether indexed evidence survives restart. A local in-memory index cannot demonstrate restart durability.\n\nThe Qdrant lab uses the real client in persistent local mode with explicitly synthetic three-coordinate vectors. This validates store mechanics and authorization filters without pretending the vectors came from a semantic model.''',
'''Create a collection with declared dimension and cosine distance, upsert alpha and beta points, then use query_points with a payload Filter requiring tenant=alpha. Close and reopen the client using the same path and repeat the query. Delete one point and assert it no longer appears.\n\nFor a server deployment, Compose supplies a persistent volume and service health checks. Local embedded mode and remote service mode have different concurrency and operational guarantees; label the mode in every artifact.''',
'''A test creates the collection in memory, closes it and falsely claims persistence. A client forgets the filter on a secondary retrieval path. Duplicate random IDs accumulate stale versions instead of replacing them.\n\nTest repeated upserts, deletion, reopen, dimension mismatch and both positive/negative tenant queries. Verify the returned payload against the authenticated scope even after the database filter.''',
'''Retain a store smoke report containing mode, collection contract, point IDs, filtered query results and restart/deletion checks. ANN quality tests are separate and require a larger labeled corpus and an exact-search reference.\n\nFor SDETs, isolate database mechanics from embedding quality. A real persistent store over synthetic vectors is honest infrastructure evidence; a semantic retrieval claim additionally requires a trained encoder and relevant-document labels.''',
"from qdrant_client import QdrantClient,models\nclient=QdrantClient(path='artifacts/qdrant')\nflt=models.Filter(must=[models.FieldCondition(key='tenant',match=models.MatchValue(value='alpha'))])\n# query_points uses the collection's declared metric\n# result=client.query_points('fixtures',query=[1.,0.,0.],query_filter=flt,limit=3)",
"def upsert(points,new):\n    by_id={p['id']:p for p in points}\n    by_id[new['id']]=new\n    return [by_id[k] for k in sorted(by_id)]",
"assert upsert([{'id':1,'v':0}],{'id':1,'v':2})==[{'id':1,'v':2}]\nassert len(upsert([{'id':1}],{'id':2}))==2","def upsert(points,new):\n    return []",
('What proves persistence rather than in-memory correctness?',['Reopening the same store and verifying data','One successful query before shutdown','A collection name'],0,'Durability requires the state to survive the process/client lifecycle.'),['Qdrant','Payload filter'])
lab(15,'citations','Context construction: resolved citations and missing evidence',
'''Context construction turns retrieved chunks into a bounded evidence package. Each chunk should carry an immutable evidence ID and enough source metadata for a citation to resolve. The answer must cite only supplied, authorized evidence; a plausible URL or title is not a valid provenance check.\n\nA no-answer case is not a retrieval exception. The service may function correctly while no evidence supports the request. In that state, abstention is a valid outcome and should be present in the evaluation dataset.''',
'''Deduplicate overlapping chunks, reserve output/instruction tokens and pack evidence according to relevance and coverage. Use explicit evidence blocks with IDs; treat their text as untrusted data. After generation, validate cited IDs against the actual packed set, then evaluate whether the cited passage supports the claim.\n\nThese are two levels: deterministic resolution checks identity, while groundedness checks semantic support. Neither a resolved ID nor a high relevance score establishes every generated statement.''',
'''The retriever finds the correct document, but context packing drops it. The model cites an ID remembered from an earlier session that was not supplied this turn. Another answer cites an authorized document that discusses the opposite rule.\n\nTest unresolved IDs, empty context, conflicting evidence and revocation. Retain retrieval and packed-context IDs separately so the first failing stage can be identified.''',
'''The extractive baseline returns authorized source text with citations, visibly labeled as extractive rather than a live LLM answer. Live generation must meet the same provenance checks and an independently calibrated support rubric.\n\nSDETs should report citation resolution, claim support and no-answer behavior separately. This makes a retrieval regression distinguishable from a hallucination or a context-budget defect.''',
"supplied={'D1','D2'}\ncited=['D1']\nassert set(cited)<=supplied",
"def citations_valid(cited,supplied,tenant,documents):\n    by_id={d['id']:d for d in documents}\n    return bool(cited) and all(i in supplied and i in by_id and by_id[i]['tenant']==tenant and not by_id[i].get('deleted') for i in cited)",
"docs=[{'id':'D1','tenant':'a'},{'id':'D2','tenant':'b'}]\nassert citations_valid(['D1'],{'D1'},'a',docs)\nassert not citations_valid(['D2'],{'D2'},'a',docs)\nassert not citations_valid([],set(),'a',docs)","def citations_valid(cited,supplied,tenant,documents):\n    return False",
('Does a resolved citation prove claim support?',['No: semantic support is a separate check','Yes: IDs guarantee meaning','Only with top-k=1'],0,'Resolution establishes source identity; the claim can still misrepresent the passage.'),['Citation resolution','Context packing'])
lab(16,'freshness','Freshness, deletion and incremental indexing',
'''A document store and its search index can diverge. Source deletion, permission revocation or a new version must propagate to retrieval before the service claims fresh authorized evidence. Incremental indexing updates changed records without rebuilding everything, but needs a consistent generation and deletion policy.\n\nFreshness is a measurable lag between source change and serving visibility. A healthy database connection says nothing about whether the current index reflects required changes.''',
'''Use source version, content hash, tenant and deleted status to compute an ingestion delta. Upsert stable IDs or replace the prior chunk set atomically; mark deleted records unavailable immediately and remove their vectors. Switch serving generations only after consistency checks.\n\nA query trace records source/index versions. Permissions are revalidated at retrieval time so stale payloads cannot authorize a revoked document. Cache invalidation includes index generation and permission scope.''',
'''New chunks are added but old chunks are never removed, producing contradictory answers. A tombstone is cleared accidentally when a retry replays an earlier ingest event. A source permission change fails to invalidate cached context.\n\nTest update ordering, duplicate events, out-of-order versions, deletion/reingestion and permission revocation. Reject an older source version when a newer version is already committed.''',
'''Define and test a freshness SLO using synthetic updates and timestamps. Retain old compatible indexes for rollback while ensuring revoked content is blocked across every generation.\n\nSDETs should inspect served IDs after changes rather than trusting ingestion success logs. A release passes only when updated/deleted evidence behaves correctly through the complete retrieval and cache path.''',
"current={'D1':{'version':2,'deleted':False}}\nincoming={'id':'D1','version':1}\nassert incoming['version']<current['D1']['version']",
"def apply_event(state,event):\n    prior=state.get(event['id'])\n    if prior and event['version']<=prior['version']:return state.copy()\n    return {**state,event['id']:{'version':event['version'],'deleted':event.get('deleted',False)}}",
"s={'D1':{'version':2,'deleted':True}}\nassert apply_event(s,{'id':'D1','version':1})==s\nassert apply_event(s,{'id':'D1','version':3,'deleted':False})['D1']['version']==3","def apply_event(state,event):\n    return state",
('What prevents a stale retry from resurrecting deleted evidence?',['Monotonic version handling and tombstone policy','A longer prompt','A higher top-k'],0,'Out-of-order ingest events must not overwrite a newer deletion state.'),['Tombstone','Incremental indexing','Freshness'])
lab(17,'ndcg','Graded ranking: calculate NDCG and inspect its limits',
'''Binary relevance treats all useful documents equally. Graded relevance can distinguish a directly answering source from a partially useful source. DCG discounts later ranks because users and context budgets prioritize earlier evidence; NDCG divides observed gain by the best possible gain at the same cutoff.\n\nA common gain function is 2^relevance−1, divided by log2(rank+1). The chosen grades, gain and cutoff are part of the metric definition. Comparing runs with different conventions is misleading.''',
'''For relevance grades [0,3,1], the observed gain puts no benefit at rank one and large benefit at rank two. Sort the same candidate grades descending to calculate an illustrative ideal denominator. In a real benchmark, the ideal uses the full labeled relevant set at K, not merely the retrieved grades.\n\nHandle an all-zero ideal explicitly, commonly returning zero. Preserve graded labels and reviewer guidance in the dataset so repeated evaluation uses the same relevance interpretation.''',
'''A scorer normalizes by the observed ranking itself, yielding one for every query. Another uses only retrieved items for the ideal and hides missing highly relevant documents. NDCG can improve while a required authorization invariant fails.\n\nTest a perfect ranking, a swapped ranking, no relevance and missing high-grade evidence. Verify labels manually on a sample because precise arithmetic cannot repair bad relevance judgments.''',
'''Use NDCG alongside Recall@K and case-level failure analysis. The metric answers ordering quality under declared relevance, while recall identifies missing evidence and safety checks enforce eligibility.\n\nFor SDETs, hand-computed small rankings are an independent oracle for the scorer. Retain numerical tolerances and metric conventions in the report rather than treating a library default as a universal definition.''',
"import math\ndef dcg(grades):return sum((2**r-1)/math.log2(i+2) for i,r in enumerate(grades))\nprint(dcg([0,3,1])/dcg([3,1,0]))",
"def ndcg(grades,ideal,k):\n    import math\n    if k<=0:raise ValueError('k')\n    def dcg(xs):return sum((2**r-1)/math.log2(i+2) for i,r in enumerate(xs[:k]))\n    best=dcg(sorted(ideal,reverse=True))\n    return dcg(grades)/best if best else 0",
"assert ndcg([3,2,1],[3,2,1],3)==1\nassert 0<ndcg([0,3,1],[3,1,0],3)<1\nassert ndcg([0],[0],1)==0\nassert ndcg([1],[3,1],1)<1","def ndcg(grades,ideal,k):\n    return 0",
('Where should the ideal ranking come from?',['The full labeled relevant set at the same cutoff','The observed ranking unchanged','Only unauthorized neighbors'],0,'The ideal denominator must reflect available gold relevance, including relevant items retrieval missed.'),['DCG','Graded relevance'])
lab(17,'judges','Judge calibration: agreement, false accepts and bias',
'''A semantic evaluator is another model-based component with its own error distribution. Human labels establish the intended rubric on a calibration set. Agreement measures how often judge and human decisions match, while false-accept rate measures unsafe or wrong outputs that the judge passes.\n\nJudge choice, prompt, rubric and threshold are versioned dependencies. A familiar framework such as Ragas or DeepEval supplies metrics and integrations, but the application still owns calibration and release meaning.''',
'''Build a reviewed set with clearly supported, unsupported, incomplete and contradictory answers. Record human pass/fail and judge score, then sweep thresholds and inspect false accepts/false rejects. Use the same cases to compare evaluator versions, keeping a separate set for confirmation.\n\nPairwise comparison must randomize answer order and measure position sensitivity. Evaluate self-preference and verbosity effects using controlled candidates differing only in style or provenance.''',
'''A judge passes a verbose answer with one false claim and fails a concise correct abstention. Its rubric asks for confidence rather than evidence support. Evaluator errors are averaged into the system score without any calibration record.\n\nInspect disagreements at claim level. Bound judge retries and treat malformed evaluator output as incomplete evidence. Never allow candidate text to become instructions to the evaluator.''',
'''A calibrated report states human sample size, agreement, false accepts, false rejects and threshold rationale. Safety invariants remain deterministic hard gates where possible, even when semantic dimensions use a judge.\n\nFor SDETs, evaluator testing is testing the test oracle. A high model score means little until you can show the scorer distinguishes known-good from known-bad cases under the intended rubric.''',
"human=[True,False,False,True]\njudge=[True,True,False,True]\nfalse_accepts=sum(j and not h for h,j in zip(human,judge))\nassert false_accepts==1",
"def judge_errors(human,judge):\n    if len(human)!=len(judge) or not human:raise ValueError('paired labels')\n    return {'agreement':sum(h==j for h,j in zip(human,judge))/len(human),'false_accepts':sum(j and not h for h,j in zip(human,judge)),'false_rejects':sum(h and not j for h,j in zip(human,judge))}",
"assert judge_errors([True,False],[True,True])=={'agreement':.5,'false_accepts':1,'false_rejects':0}\ntry:judge_errors([],[]);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def judge_errors(human,judge):\n    return {}",
('Why test the evaluator on known-bad answers?',['To measure false accepts and rubric validity','To train the final test set into the application','To increase average verbosity'],0,'Evaluation must establish that the oracle rejects the failure modes it claims to detect.'),['Evaluator calibration','False accept'])
